Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Vmag (apparent visual magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) tend to be closer or intrinsically brighter. Giants often have lower absolute magnitude but may have a range of apparent magnitudes depending on distance. Alone, Vmag is not a strong indicator of giant status without distance info.

- Plx (parallax): Larger parallax means closer star. Giants can be either close or far. Parallax helps determine distance and thus absolute magnitude.

- e_Plx (error in parallax): High error reduces confidence in distance and derived absolute magnitude.

- B-V (color index): Indicates star temperature/color. Giants often have specific color ranges depending on spectral type; e.g., red giants have higher B-V (redder), blue giants lower B-V.

- SpType (spectral type): This is a strong indicator. The luminosity class in spectral type (e.g., III for giants, V for main-sequence dwarfs) directly relates to whether a star is a giant.

- Amag (absolute magnitude): Giants generally have lower absolute magnitudes (brighter) than dwarfs of the same spectral type. High Amag values (very faint) suggest non-giants.

